ple who a lot of people know who a  lot of people look at in our school the  clique leaders on the other hand are at  the center of social groups that may not  be as mainstream oftentimes the word  clique is associated with popularity but  here what we mean is that these students  may stick to themselves in the school  these students are often also not as  much notice by teachers when it comes to  choosing student leaders for change  efforts at a school what's important  about reaching out to these clique  leaders as well as to the widely known  students is that cliques are very  well-known for defining their identity  as against the mainstream of the school  so if you wanted to reach out to the  widest number of students at the school  you'd want to reach out to these  students as well to their leaders to try  to convince them that yes we are also  participating in these new standards so  how would we go about testing this idea  that social reference can spread a new  ideas about social standards of behavior  and behavior itself being behavioral  scientists we didn't experiment  we went into a high school and we  measured their social network as I just  described to you earlier and we  identified the students were widely  known the students were clique leaders  and we randomly selected some of them to  participate in a year-long campaign  against conflict at the school others  who weren't select  did simply didn't participate in the  program there was no intervention for  these students and what we did is over  time over the course of the year as  students participated in this campaign  by associating their faces with slogans  that they had designed to try to reduce  these kinds of behaviors at their school  at the end of the school year we tracked  the students who were connected to these  social reference verses the students who  were connected to the social reference  who had not participated and the bottom  line is that we find that they in fact  are very influential over the people to  whom they are connected so when we asked  students at the end of the year whether  it's normal to start drama at their  school starting drama is their way of  talking about bullying for example one  way of talking about bullying at this  school students who have been connected  to these these social reference were  significantly less likely to say that it  was normal they also communicated other  kinds of social standards for example  it's okay to ignore rumors they were  much more likely to say this if they  were connected in the network to one of  these students but it wasn't just their  perceptions it was also their behavior  that changed so we gathered  administrative data at the school about  whether these students had been  disciplined for peer conflict we  surveyed teachers in separate surveys to  ask them are these students contributing  to a negative environment and turned out  that those who were connected to these  social referenced their behavior was  also likely to change they were less  likely to be nominated by teachers for  bad behavior more likely to be nominated  as students who would even defend others  from harassment so what this shows us is  that social reference can have a  powerful influence on the students to  whom they are connected but the question  is did they have an effect on the  climate the entire climate of the school  and that's something that we couldn't  answer with this research what we needed  to do is to be able to compare this  school in which we were intervening to a  school in which we weren't present the  program wasn't there at all and with the  support of C far this is it in fact what  we did this year we went into 656  different middle schools in New Jersey  and measured the networks of every  single school which involved 24,000  children we then went into half of these  schools randomly selected and conducted  a program in which we invited their  schools social reference students to  participate in another campaign against  conflict at their school another twist  that we that we used in this particular  experiment is that we invited varying  amounts of non social reference to also  participate in in the program so that we  could test not just can social reference  change the conflict the the climate of  conflict at their entire school overall  levels and patterns of student conflict  but are they necessary can other  students do this as well what is their  contribution so first what they did all  of these students was decide what they  did in fact want to change about their  school and our program was called the  routes program I mentioned this just  because it's an important point again  about student social identity many  interventions will ask students to  repeat phrases about conflict that are  that have been delivered to them by  adults and what we thought was that the  messages about social standards would be  much more authentic if they were coming  from students who had decided for  themselves  what is it that we really want to change  what's going on at our school and these  are some examples of things that  students brought up as things that they  would like to change about their school  some of you some of them you might  recognize as classic stories of bullying  that adults talk about in the media but  many of them were not but still they  were valid they were of concern to these  students they were causing them distress  from there where the students did was  launch their own campaign they had lots  of different strategies for doing so  including designing slogans and signing  their names to it again to make very  salient that they were the ones who were  promoting this kind of new social  standard they gave out wristbands with  some of the slogan words on them when  they recognized someone at their school  doing something good as a reward and as  an extra motivation so what did we find  well first of all they were extremely  successful at getting students at their  schools to talk about conflict and what  you see in this graph is just a higher  level overall independent of how many  social references were part of the group  and getting to people at the school to  talk  conflicts compared again to the schools  where we weren't doing this intervention  program but here's a critical graph at  each school we also gathered  disciplinary data to ask how much  overall peer conflict was there at the  school we these are data actually from  the school's administrative records and  what you see here is that again there is  an effect of having this intervention at  your school there was lower overall peer  conflicts in such a way that you could  maybe suggest that this was an entire  climate change this effect size is  equivalent to every single student in an  intervention school having one less  incident of getting pulled into the  office for conflicts over the course of  the school year but well you also notice  about this graph is that the difference  between intervention and treatment and  the intervention and the no intervention  schools grows the more social reference  you add to the group so the social  reference are the ones who are driving  in part this behavioral change having  more of them having at least a quarter  of them is what leads to this kind of  overall school change so I'd like to end  there and and say that for one I think  that one emerging lesson from this  research which is still ongoing is a  very exciting one which suggests that  its students themselves who can be  involved in setting the standards of a  school climate note that we did nothing  to change some of the larger factors of  climate over the course of this study we  didn't train the teachers we didn't  change the administrative structure or  any of the rules this was all due to  student changed behavior over the course  of of the year the other thing I want to  point out is that what this what this  research suggests it redirects our  attention from these larger scale  factors of a school to more subtle  factors of social perception and social  identity these are in fact what we would  like to suggest the cult the sort of  psychological underpinnings of culture  how we understand the social standards  of our environment another exciting  thing is that while we can identify  certain social reference in a group who  can make these changes it wasn't  necessarily an elite  task force of social reference students  who accomplish these kinds of  large-scale behavioral change at their  schools the most the the highest  proportion of social reference in these  intervention groups at the schools was a  third so in fact what these students did  was build an alliance of students who  were willing to help who were not  necessarily at the center of their  social networks along with students who  were in fact some of the more salient  leaders of their school so I'd like to  leave you with those thoughts and to say  finally that this kind of research  doesn't disregard the importance of  large scale factors but it does redirect  our attention to some of the ways in  which social identity and social  perceptions can change these large  scales of climates of culture in our  schools and and perhaps elsewhere thank  you  you

