ects our  attention from these larger scale  factors of a school to more subtle  factors of social perception and social  identity these are in fact what we would  like to suggest the cult the sort of  psychological underpinnings of culture  how we understand the social standards  of our environment another exciting  thing is that while we can identify  certain social reference in a group who  can make these changes it wasn't  necessarily an elite  task force of social reference students  who accomplish these kinds of  large-scale behavioral change at their  schools the most the the highest  proportion of social reference in these  intervention groups at the schools was a  third so in fact what these students did  was build an alliance of students who  were willing to help who were not  necessarily at the center of their  social networks along with students who  were in fact some of the more salient  leaders of their school so I'd like to  leave you with those thoughts and to say  finally that this kind of research  doesn't disregard the importance of  large scale factors but it does redirect  our attention to some of the ways in  which social identity and social  perceptions can change these large  scales of climates of culture in our  schools and and perhaps elsewhere thank  you  you
 

